    "updated_at": "2026-11-20T17:16:22Z",
    "body": "**Test plan on a real WAZER** (mkinney, 2026-11-20: there is no air-cut mode known; \"once the jet is on, it cuts\"; files go to the machine on an SD card).\n\nA ladder of short test files, each its own file on the SD card, each run only after the one before looks right:\n\n1. **Motion only (dry run):** the same program with every jet, pump and abrasive command left out (no `M3`/`M8`, no pierce dwell). The machine initialises (`M1403`), shows the cut extents (`M1405`/`M1406`), moves the head over the path at cut feed, and shuts down (`M1404`). It checks direction, scale, extents and that the firmware accepts the file, with nothing cut. The post gets a `dry run` option that writes this variant, with a header line `(DRY RUN - NO JET)`.\n2. **Water only, on cardboard or thin plastic:** the jet on, the abrasive off (if the firmware separates them). Water alone marks or cuts soft material, so the shape shows without abrasive wear.\n3. **Full cut on scrap:** a small square with one hole in thin scrap (for example 1-2 mm aluminium or acrylic), with lead-ins and the pierce dwell.\n\nEvidence welcome before step 1: one WAM-made G-code file of a simple square from the owner's SD card, read only as evidence of the dialect the firmware accepts (the same way a sample program was read for #1907); no WAM code is copied.\n\nDone for the test: steps 1-3 pass on the owner's machine, recorded on this issue.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
